%% Chapter 36: Top-Down Parsing
\label{ch:top-down-parsing}

\section{Introduction to Top-Down Parsing}
\label{sec:top-down-intro}

Top-down parsing constructs a parse tree from the root (start symbol) down to the leaves (input tokens). At each step, the parser expands a nonterminal by choosing one of its productions, attempting to match the right-hand side against the input. The most intuitive form of top-down parsing is recursive descent, where each nonterminal is implemented as a function that calls other functions for the nonterminals in its productions.

The key challenge of top-down parsing is choosing the right production at each step. For LL(1) grammars, this choice can be made by looking at only one token of lookahead. More powerful variants (LL(k), LL(*)) use more lookahead or adaptive strategies.

\begin{historical}
Top-down parsing was first studied systematically in the 1960s. The LL(1) parsing technique was described by Lewis and Stearns (1968) and by Aho and Ullman (1972). Recursive descent parsers were used in early compiler implementations but were not formalized until later. The LL(*) parsing algorithm was developed by Parr and Quong (1995) and implemented in ANTLR. Modern parser generators such as ANTLR v4 use adaptive LL(*) parsing, which combines the simplicity of recursive descent with the power of general parsing.
\end{historical}

\section{Recursive Descent Parsing}
\label{sec:recursive-descent}

\subsection{Basic Recursive Descent}
\label{sec:basic-rd}

In recursive descent parsing, each nonterminal $A$ is implemented as a function \texttt{parse\_A()} that attempts to match the input against one of $A$'s productions.

\begin{example}[Recursive Descent for Expressions]
\label{ex:rd-expr}
For the grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Expression Grammar}
  E & \longrightarrow T \; E' \\
  E' & \longrightarrow + \; T \; E' \mid \epsilon \\
  T & \longrightarrow F \; T' \\
  T' & \longrightarrow \times \; F \; T' \mid \epsilon \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}

The recursive descent parser is:
\begin{verbatim}
  void parse_E() { parse_T(); parse_Eprime(); }
  void parse_Eprime() {
      if (lookahead == PLUS) {
          match(PLUS); parse_T(); parse_Eprime();
      }
  }
  void parse_T() { parse_F(); parse_Tprime(); }
  void parse_Tprime() {
      if (lookahead == TIMES) {
          match(TIMES); parse_F(); parse_Tprime();
      }
  }
  void parse_F() {
      if (lookahead == LPAREN) {
          match(LPAREN); parse_E(); match(RPAREN);
      } else if (lookahead == ID) {
          match(ID);
      } else { error(); }
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Predictive Parsing}
\label{sec:predictive}

A \emph{predictive parser} is a recursive descent parser that never needs to backtrack. It can always determine which production to use by looking at the next input token (the lookahead).

\begin{definition}[Predictive Parser]
\label{def:predictive}
A \textbf{predictive parser} is a top-down parser that, at each step, selects the correct production based solely on the current nonterminal and the lookahead token, without backtracking.
\end{definition}

\section{LL(1) Grammars and Parsing Tables}
\label{sec:ll1}

\subsection{First and Follow Sets}
\label{sec:first-follow}

The construction of LL(1) parsing tables requires computing \textbf{First} and \textbf{Follow} sets.

\begin{definition}[First Set]
\label{def:first}
The \textbf{First set} of a string $\alpha$, written $\first{\alpha}$, is the set of terminals that can begin strings derived from $\alpha$:
\[
\first{\alpha} = \{a \in T \mid \alpha \mderives a\beta \text{ for some } \beta\}
\]
If $\alpha \mderives \epsilon$, then $\epsilon \in \first{\alpha}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Follow Set]
\label{def:follow}
The \textbf{Follow set} of a variable $A$, written $\follow{A}$, is the set of terminals that can appear immediately after $A$ in some sentential form:
\[
\follow{A} = \{a \in T \mid S \mderives \alpha A a \beta \text{ for some } \alpha, \beta\}
\]
If $A$ can be the rightmost symbol in some sentential form, then $\$ \in \follow{A}$ (where $\$$ is the end-of-input marker).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Computing First and Follow Sets}
\label{sec:compute-first-follow}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\For{each production $A \to \alpha$}
  \If{$\alpha = \epsilon$}
    \State $\first{A} \gets \first{A} \cup \{\epsilon\}$
  \Else
    \State $\first{A} \gets \first{A} \cup (\first{\alpha} \setminus \{\epsilon\})$
    \If{$\epsilon \in \first{\alpha}$}
      \State $\first{A} \gets \first{A} \cup \{\epsilon\}$
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Repeat until no changes
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{LL(1) Condition}
\label{sec:ll1-condition}

\begin{definition}[LL(1) Grammar]
\label{def:ll1}
A grammar $G$ is \textbf{LL(1)} if and only if for every pair of distinct productions $A \to \alpha$ and $A \to \beta$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item At most one of $\alpha$ and $\beta$ can derive strings beginning with the same terminal
  \item If $\alpha \mderives \epsilon$, then $\beta$ cannot derive any string beginning with a terminal in $\follow{A}$
  \item If $\beta \mderives \epsilon$, then $\alpha$ cannot derive any string beginning with a terminal in $\follow{A}$
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently: $\first{\alpha} \cap \first{\beta} = \emptyset$, and if $\nullable{\alpha}$ then $\first{\beta} \cap \follow{A} = \emptyset$, and vice versa.
\end{definition}

\subsection{LL(1) Parsing Tables}
\label{sec:ll1-tables}

\begin{definition}[LL(1) Parsing Table]
\label{def:ll1-table}
The \textbf{LL(1) parsing table} $M[A, a]$ is defined as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For each production $A \to \alpha$ and each $a \in \first{\alpha}$: $M[A, a] = A \to \alpha$
  \item If $\epsilon \in \first{\alpha}$, then for each $b \in \follow{A}$: $M[A, b] = A \to \alpha$
\end{itemize}
If any entry $M[A, a]$ has multiple productions, the grammar is not LL(1).
\end{definition}

\subsection{LL(1) Parsing Algorithm}
\label{sec:ll1-algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Parse input $w\$$ using LL(1) table $M$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Push $\$$ and $S$ onto the stack
\State $\texttt{ip} \gets 0$ \Comment{input pointer}
\Loop
  \State $X \gets$ top of stack
  \State $a \gets w[\texttt{ip}]$
  \If{$X = a = \$$}
    \State \textbf{accept}
  \ElsIf{$X = a \neq \$$}
    \State Pop $X$; $\texttt{ip} \gets \texttt{ip} + 1$
  \ElsIf{$X \in T$}
    \State \textbf{error}
  \ElsIf{$M[X, a] = X \to Y_1 Y_2 \cdots Y_k$}
    \State Pop $X$; Push $Y_k, Y_{k-1}, \ldots, Y_1$
    \State Output production $X \to Y_1 Y_2 \cdots Y_k$
  \Else
    \State \textbf{error}
  \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Left Recursion Elimination}
\label{sec:left-recursion}

\subsection{The Problem with Left Recursion}
\label{sec:left-recursion-problem}

Top-down parsers cannot handle left-recursive grammars. A production $A \to A \alpha$ causes the parser for $A$ to call itself immediately, resulting in infinite recursion.

\subsection{Eliminating Immediate Left Recursion}
\label{sec:eliminate-immediate}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Transform $A \to A \alpha_1 \mid A \alpha_2 \mid \cdots \mid \beta_1 \mid \beta_2 \mid \cdots$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Replace with:
\State $A \to \beta_1 A' \mid \beta_2 A' \mid \cdots$
\State $A' \to \alpha_1 A' \mid \alpha_2 A' \mid \cdots \mid \epsilon$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Left Recursion Elimination]
\label{ex:left-rec-elim}
The left-recursive grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Left-Recursive}
  E & \longrightarrow E + T \mid T
\end{grammar}
becomes:
\begin{grammar}{Right-Factored}
  E & \longrightarrow T \; E' \\
  E' & \longrightarrow + \; T \; E' \mid \epsilon
\end{grammar}
\end{example}

\subsection{Eliminating General Left Recursion}
\label{sec:eliminate-general}

For grammars with indirect left recursion ($A \mderives^+ A \alpha$), a more general algorithm is required. The algorithm processes nonterminals in a topological order and systematically removes left-recursive paths.

\section{LL(k) and Predictive Parsing}
\label{sec:llk}

\subsection{LL(k) Grammars}
\label{sec:llk-grammars}

\begin{definition}[LL(k) Grammar]
\label{def:llk}
A grammar is \textbf{LL(k)} if the parsing decision (which production to use) can be made by looking at the current nonterminal and the next $k$ input tokens.
\end{definition}

Increasing $k$ makes more grammars parsable but increases the size of the parsing table exponentially ($|T|^k$ entries per nonterminal).

\subsection{LL(*) Parsing}
\label{sec:llstar}

LL(*) parsing, used in ANTLR v3, extends LL(1) by allowing unbounded lookahead. The parser analyzes the grammar to determine how much lookahead is needed for each decision.

\begin{definition}[LL(*) Decision]
\label{def:llstar}
An \textbf{LL(*) decision} is resolved by constructing a DFA that reads input tokens and determines which production to use. The DFA is constructed automatically from the grammar.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Adaptive LL(*) Parsing}
\label{sec:allstar}

ANTLR v4 uses \emph{adaptive LL(*)} (ALL(*)) parsing, which constructs the lookahead DFA at parse time rather than at grammar analysis time. This allows the parser to adapt to the specific input being parsed.

\section{Error Reporting in Top-Down Parsers}
\label{sec:td-errors}

\subsection{Error Detection}
\label{sec:td-error-detection}

In a predictive parser, an error is detected when the current input token does not match any expected token:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The top of the stack is a terminal that doesn't match the input
  \item The parsing table entry $M[A, a]$ is empty
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Error Recovery}
\label{sec:td-error-recovery}

Common error recovery strategies for top-down parsers:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Panic mode}: Skip input tokens until a synchronizing token is found (typically from the Follow set of the current nonterminal)
  \item \textbf{Phrase-level recovery}: Insert or delete tokens to continue parsing
  \item \textbf{Error productions}: Add productions that explicitly handle common errors
\end{itemize}

\section{Parser Generators: ANTLR and JavaCC}
\label{sec:td-generators}

\subsection{ANTLR}
\label{sec:antlr}

ANTLR (ANother Tool for Language Recognition) is a powerful parser generator that reads a grammar and generates a parser in Java, C\#, Python, JavaScript, or other target languages. ANTLR v4 uses ALL(*) parsing and generates recursive descent parsers directly.

\begin{verbatim}
  grammar Expr;
  
  prog: stat+ ;
  stat: expr NEWLINE        # printExpr
      | ID '=' expr NEWLINE # assign
      | NEWLINE             # blank
      ;
  expr: expr op=('*'|'/') expr  # MulDiv
      | expr op=('+'|'-') expr  # AddSub
      | INT                     # int
      | ID                      # id
      | '(' expr ')'            # parens
      ;
  
  MUL : '*' ;
  ADD : '+' ;
  ID  : [a-zA-Z]+ ;
  INT : [0-9]+ ;
  NEWLINE: '\r'? '\n' ;
  WS  : [ \t]+ -> skip ;
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{JavaCC}
\label{sec:javacc}

JavaCC is a parser generator for Java that supports LL(k) parsing with syntactic lookahead. It generates Java source code for the parser.

\section{Practical LL(1) Grammar Design}
\label{sec:practical-ll1}

\subsection{Designing for LL(1)}
\label{sec:design-ll1}

When designing grammars for LL(1) parsing, several guidelines should be followed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Eliminate left recursion}: Transform left-recursive productions into right-recursive form.
  \item \textbf{Left-factor common prefixes}: If two alternatives for a nonterminal begin with the same symbol, factor out the common prefix.
  \item \textbf{Avoid ambiguity}: Use grammar structure to encode precedence and associativity.
  \item \textbf{Minimize epsilon productions}: Epsilon productions require careful handling of Follow sets.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Left Factoring]
\label{ex:left-factoring}
The grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Before Left Factoring}
  S & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \textbf{ else } S
\end{grammar}
has a common prefix \textbf{if} $E$ \textbf{then} $S$. Left-factoring produces:
\begin{grammar}{After Left Factoring}
  S & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \; S' \\
  S' & \longrightarrow \textbf{else } S \mid \epsilon
\end{grammar}
However, this still has a FIRST/FOLLOW conflict for $S'$ (since \textbf{else} $\in \first{S'} \cap \follow{S'}$). This is the classic ``dangling else'' problem, which requires additional disambiguation.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Dangling Else Problem}
\label{sec:dangling-else}

The dangling else problem is the classic example of an LL(1) conflict. In the grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Dangling Else}
  S & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \textbf{ else } S \\
    & \longrightarrow \text{other}
\end{grammar}

The input \texttt{if E then if E then S else S} can be parsed two ways:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \textbf{else} matches the first \textbf{if}
  \item The \textbf{else} matches the second \textbf{if} (the standard interpretation)
\end{itemize}

The standard resolution is to match the \textbf{else} with the nearest unmatched \textbf{if}. In LL(1) parsing, this can be achieved by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Using a non-LL(1) grammar and resolving the conflict in favor of the shift (matching the else with the nearest if)
  \item Restructuring the grammar to make the standard interpretation the only valid parse
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Building LL(1) Parsing Tables: A Complete Example}
\label{sec:ll1-complete-example}

Consider the following grammar for simple statements:
\begin{grammar}{Statement Grammar}
  S & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} := E \mid \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S \mid \textbf{while } E \textbf{ do } S \mid \{ L \} \\
  L & \longrightarrow S \; L' \\
  L' & \longrightarrow ; \; S \; L' \mid \epsilon \\
  E & \longrightarrow T \; E' \\
  E' & \longrightarrow + \; T \; E' \mid \epsilon \\
  T & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \mid \textbf{num}
\end{grammar}

The LL(1) parsing table for this grammar has entries for each (nonterminal, terminal) pair. For example:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $M[S, \textbf{id}] = S \to \textbf{id} := E$
  \item $M[S, \textbf{if}] = S \to \textbf{if } E \textbf{ then } S$
  \item $M[S, \textbf{while}] = S \to \textbf{while } E \textbf{ do } S$
  \item $M[S, \{] = S \to \{ L \}$
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Topics in Top-Down Parsing}
\label{sec:advanced-td}

\subsection{Memoization in Recursive Descent}
\label{sec:td-memoization}

Recursive descent parsers can be enhanced with memoization to avoid redundant parsing. This is the basis of packrat parsing (see \cref{ch:parser-combinators}).

\begin{definition}[Memoized Recursive Descent]
\label{def:memoized-rd}
A \textbf{memoized recursive descent} parser caches the result of each parsing function at each input position. Before parsing, the parser checks the cache; if a result is found, it is returned immediately without re-parsing.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Infinite Lookahead and Termination}
\label{sec:infinite-lookahead}

When using unbounded lookahead (as in LL(*) or ALL(*)), the lookahead DFA may not terminate for certain grammars. ANTLR handles this by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Detecting non-termination during DFA construction
  \item Limiting the depth of lookahead exploration
  \item Falling back to a default alternative when the DFA cannot decide
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Semantic Predicates}
\label{sec:semantic-predicates}

Semantic predicates allow the parser to make decisions based on information beyond the grammar structure:

\begin{verbatim}
  declaration
      : { isTypeName(LT(1).text) }? ID '(' paramList ')'  // function declaration
      | ID '(' argList ')'                                 // function call
      ;
\end{verbatim}

The predicate \texttt{isTypeName(LT(1).text)} is evaluated during parsing to determine whether the identifier is a type name (function declaration) or a variable (function call).

