\section{Reporting Expected Tokens from the Pending Stack}

An LL parser can report a more precise expected-token set than the set of
populated cells in the current table row. A row combines contexts: a nullable
nonterminal can have a reduction entry for a token that follows it somewhere
else in the grammar. At a failure, the predictive stack records which symbols
actually remain in this invocation. To compute its candidate tokens, walk
from the top down, adding the terminal-only FIRST set of each nonterminal,
and continue past that nonterminal only if it can disappear.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Pending stack $\gamma$, top first; end marker at bottom
\Require Nullability and terminal-only FIRST sets
\State $E\gets\varnothing$
\ForAll{$X$ in $\gamma$ from top to bottom}
  \If{$X$ is a terminal or the end marker}
    \State $E\gets E\cup\{X\}$ \Comment{A literal cannot disappear}
    \State \Return $E$
  \EndIf
  \State $E\gets E\cup\mathrm{FIRST}(X)$
  \If{$X$ is not nullable}
    \State \Return $E$ \Comment{No deeper symbol can appear first}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $E$
\end{sudocode}

Consider $S\to \texttt{(}\,A\,\texttt{)}\mid
\texttt{[}\,A\,\texttt{]}$ and $A\to\texttt{id}\mid\epsilon$.
After shifting \texttt{(}, the pending sequence is $A$,
\texttt{)}, end marker. If the lookahead is a comma, the diagnostic
reports \texttt{id} or \texttt{)}. A lookup of the populated cells for
$A$ would also suggest \texttt{]}, because the square-bracket
production puts it in FOLLOW$(A)$. Inside parentheses that token cannot
legally begin the pending sequence. With only the end marker pending,
the same algorithm reports end of input.

The invariant after skipping a prefix is that the prefix can derive
the empty string and $E$ contains exactly the tokens that can begin a
nonempty derivation of the skipped prefix, assuming a reduced grammar
and ordinary context-free productions. No FOLLOW lookup is needed
because the surrounding context is already on the stack. With bitset
FIRST sets the cost is one union per visited nonterminal, paid only on
an error. These are next-token expectations, not repair suggestions:
a token in the set might require several later changes to complete the
input, and predicates in a richer parser can further restrict the set.

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:td-summary}

Top-down parsing is the most intuitive parsing approach, directly mirroring the structure of the grammar. Key concepts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Recursive descent as the basic implementation strategy
  \item LL(1) grammars and parsing tables for predictive parsing
  \item First and Follow sets for grammar analysis
  \item Left recursion elimination for compatibility with top-down parsing
  \item LL(k) and LL(*) for more powerful lookahead
  \item Modern tools like ANTLR that combine recursive descent with adaptive lookahead
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The evolution of top-down parsing from simple recursive descent to adaptive LL(*) reflects the ongoing tension between simplicity and power. Key milestones include: recursive descent parsers (1960s); LL(1) parsing tables (Lewis and Stearns, 1968); LL(k) parsing (Aho and Ullman, 1972); LL(*) and ANTLR v3 (Parr and Quong, 1995); ALL(*) and ANTLR v4 (Parr et al., 2013). The development of ANTLR has been particularly influential, making top-down parsing practical for a wide range of language processing tasks.
\end{historical}
